      "error_message": "[roguelike_loop_test] printed PASS while a SCRIPT ERROR fired: \"Invalid access to property or key '_phase' on a base object of type 'previously freed'\" — exit 0 but the level-up/card-pick assertions never actually ran.",
      "file": "tests/roguelike_loop_test.gd",
      "root_cause": "GDScript coroutine functions (containing `await`) called WITHOUT `await` at the call site only run synchronously up to their first suspend point, then return immediately — they do not block the caller. `_ready()` called `_drive_starter_pick(scene)` (which internally called `_force_level_up(scene)`, which awaits `get_tree().process_frame`) as a plain statement, so `_ready()` proceeded straight to `scene.queue_free()` + `_finish()` before the suspended coroutines resumed. The background coroutines later resumed and touched the freed `scene`, and — more importantly — `_finish()` had already evaluated `_failures` (empty at that point) and printed a false PASS with exit 0.",
      "fix": "Add `await` at every call site of a function that itself contains an `await` (`await _drive_starter_pick(scene)`, `await _force_level_up(scene)`), so `_ready()` genuinely waits for the whole chain to finish before freeing the scene and reporting PASS/FAIL. Also replaced a single `await get_tree().process_frame` assumption (that _process() reacts within exactly one frame) with a small bounded poll loop (up to 10 frames) before asserting the level-up draft opened, since the exact frame the pending_levelups check lands on relative to the process_frame signal isn't a timing guarantee worth hardcoding.",
      "tags": [
        "gdscript",
        "async-await",
        "coroutine",
        "test-reliability",
        "false-positive",
        "roguelike"
      ],
